solution_steps:
To find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of two numbers, we can use the Euclidean algorithm.
First, we divide $n$ by $m$ to find the remainder:
\[
\begin{array}{c|cc}
\multicolumn{3}{r}{444444444} \\
\cline{2-3}
22222222 \hspace{0.2cm} \big) & 999999998 & \quad \text{($n/m$)} \\
\multicolumn{3}{r}{-888888888} \\
\cline{2-3}
\multicolumn{3}{r}{111111110}
\end{array}
\]
Since the remainder is not zero, we repeat the process with $m$ and the remainder $111111110$:
\[
\begin{array}{c|cc}
\multicolumn{3}{r}{22222222} \\
\cline{2-3}
111111110 \hspace{0.2cm} \big) & 22222222 & \quad \text{($m/\text{remainder}$)} \\
\multicolumn{3}{r}{-22222220} \\
\cline{2-3}
\multicolumn{3}{r}{2}
\end{array}
\]
Now the remainder is $2$, and since $2$ does not divide $m$ evenly, the gcd of $m$ and $n$ is $\boxed{2}$.
The answer is: 2

