What is the sum of $f^{-1}(7)$ and $f^{-1}(46)$ for the function $f(x)$ defined as:
\[f(x) = \left\{
\begin{array}{cl}
x + 3 & \text{if $x < 20$}, \\
2x - 2 & \text{if $x \ge 20$}.
\end{array}
\right.\]

solution_steps:
The given sum can be written as $\sum_{k=1}^{9} \frac{1}{k(k+1)}$.
We can rewrite each term as $\frac{k+1-k}{k(k+1)}=\frac{1}{k}-\frac{1}{k+1}$.
The sum then telescopes, with most terms cancelling out:
\begin{align*} \frac{1}{1}-\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{3}+\frac{1}{3}-\frac{1}{4}+\dots+\frac{1}{9}-\frac{1}{10} &= \left(\frac{1}{1}-\frac{1}{2}\right)+\left(\frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{3}\right)+\dots+\left(\frac{1}{9}-\frac{1}{10}\right) \\ &= 1-\frac{1}{10} \\ &= \boxed{\frac{9}{10}}. \end{align*}
The answer is: \frac{9}{10}

